# Cultural Landscape Report, Treatment & Management Plan: Little Round Top (National Park Service; Einhorn Yaffee Prescott, Rhodeside & Harwell, Hunter Research; 2 March 2012)
# Read: PDF pp. 19-21 (tracts and woods on the eve of the battle), 25-27 (the second day, the night of July 2, what the battle changed, the walls restacked 1882-87),
#       29 (Warren maps as the source for the battle-era features), 84 (stone breastworks and fences as described in 2010-11).
# The text layer lost the ti and tt glyphs (Ge ysburg, Na onal); every quote is typed with the groups ti tt tf ft tl ff ignored on both sides (text_defect ligature).
# Chapter 3 describes the conditions of 2010-11: nearly all breastworks extant today have been rebuilt or restored several times and are NOT 1863 profiles.
@ NPS-CLR-LRT

%basis GNMP, Research Notes on Title Searches and Deed Traces, 2010 (cited on the page)
19 | woodlots | Little Round Top slopes: a wood lot, or once cleared possibly pasture | - | pre-1863 | stated | The slopes of Little Round Top met neither condition of the early farm nuclei (fresh water, arable soil), so their value was mostly, if not entirely, as wood lot, or once cleared possibly as pasture | =The slopes of Little Round Top met neither of these conditions, so its value was mostly, if not entirely, as wood lot, or once cleared possibly as pasture.
19 | woodlots | Little Round Top slopes as rear wood lots of the Taneytown Road farms | - | pre-1863 | stated | Deeds issued after 1797 are consistent with the slopes of Little Round Top serving as the rear wood lots to farms located on flatter, more arable lowland along Taneytown Road to the east | =Deeds issued after 1797 are consistent with a pattern of land use in which the slopes of Little Round Top were serving as the rear wood lots to farms located on flatter more arable lowland along Taneytown Road to the east.
19 | woodlots | Hoke tract (1808) kept as a wood lot | - | pre-1863 | hedged | By 1808 Conrad Hoke had acquired a 187-acre tract that included the approximately 40 acres of the summit and slopes of Little Round Top, and most available evidence suggests that Hoke maintained Little Round Top as wood lot | =By 1808, Conrad Hoke had acquired a 187-acre tract that was inclusive of the approximately 40 acres that makes up the summit and slopes of Little Round Top. Most available evidence suggests that Hoke maintained Little Round Top as wood lot, a common practice on well-managed farms since farmers never wanted to be without a source of wood, used as fuel and construction material.
19 | woodlots | Hickory, chestnut and oak stands harvested before the battle | - | pre-1863 | hedged | By the early 19th century the stands of hickory, chestnut and oak on Little Round Top had likely been harvested several times over as farmers took away select mature trees and allowed younger trees to grow to maturity | =By the early 19th century, the stands of hickory, chestnut and oak on Little Round Top had likely been harvested several times over as farmers took away select mature trees and allowed younger trees to grow to maturity.
19 | woodlots | Three tracts held the slopes on the eve of the battle | - | 1863-07 | stated | On the eve of the battle the slopes of Little Round Top were held within three tracts: a 20-acre Bushman tract (bought 1861, northeast slope), an 82-acre Weikert tract (bought 1840, southeast slope) and a 29-acre Hanaway tract (bought 1858, most of the north and west slope to east of Plum Run) | =On the eve of the battle, the slopes of Little Round Top were held within three tracts: a 20-acre tract purchased by George Bushman in 1861 as part of his property on Taneytown Road, which included the northeast slope of Little Round Top; an 82-acre tract purchased by Jacob Weikert as part of his farm on Taneytown Road in 1840, which included the southeast slope of Little Round Top; and a 29-acre tract purchased by Ephraim Hanaway in 1858 including most of the north and west slope of Little Round Top to east of Plum Run.
19 | woodlots | Bushman and Weikert tracts on the east slopes wooded in July 1863 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Bushman and Weikert tracts on the east slopes of Little Round Top were wooded in July 1863 | =Critical to the battle, the Bushman and Weikert tracts on the east slopes of Little Round Top were wooded in July 1863, but | #s9
19 | woodlots | Hanaway tract (north and west slope) mostly cleared of trees | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Hanaway tract (the north and west slope of Little Round Top) had been mostly cleared of trees, opening the view from the summit over Houck's Ridge and the ground lying to its north and west | =the Hanaway tract had been mostly cleared of trees opening the view from the summit of Little Round Top over Houck’s Ridge and the ground lying to its north and west (Figure 2.08). | #s9
%basis -

21 | woodlots | When the west slope was cleared is not confirmed | - | 1863-07 | conflict | Many later secondary sources say the timber was cleared the year before the battle, but this is not confirmed by any known primary source; photographs taken after the battle show considerable secondary growth (various shrubs) and some scattered medium-size oaks and pines, and it seems likely that substantial portions of the hill had been cleared some years before the battle, perhaps as early as 1858 when Hanaway acquired the land | =Many subsequent secondary sources have stated that the timber was cleared the year before the battle but this has not been confirmed by any known primary sources. Photographs taken after the battle show a considerable amount of secondary growth (various shrubs) as well as some scattered medium-size oaks and pines (Figure 2.09). It seems likely that substantial portions of the hill had been cleared some years prior to the battle, perhaps as early as 1858 when Hanaway acquired the land.
21 | woodlots | Bushman-Weikert farm woods gave cover and a staging area | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The Bushman-Weikert farm woods on the east side of Little Round Top offered cover and a staging area for Union troops | =The Bushman-Weikert farm woods on the east side of Little Round Top offered cover and a staging area for Union troops.

25 | general | Broken, rocky and rough summit as defensive cover | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | The defense of Little Round Top was aided materially by the broken, rocky and rough summit, which offered formidable defensive cover that one Confederate officer called 'the strongest natural position I ever saw' | =The defense of Little Round Top was aided materially by the broken, rocky and rough summit, which offered formidable defensive cover that one Confederate officer called “the strongest natural position I ever saw.”
25 | roads | Wood road on the northeast slope | - | 1863-07-02 | hedged | Hazlett's Battery reached the summit, likely making use of a rough 'wood road', perhaps used for lumbering, on the northeast slope | =Hazlett’s Battery also reached the summit, likely making use of a rough “wood road,” perhaps used for lumbering, on the northeast slope.
25 | roads | Location of the wood road never documented | - | 1863-07-02 | stated | Although the wood road is mentioned in several firsthand accounts of the battle, its exact location was never documented | =Although mention of this wood road is made in several firsthand accounts of the battle, its exact location was never documented.
25 | earthworks | Stone breastworks dug in on the night after the second day | E-14,E-13 | 1863-07-02 (night) | stated | That night Union forces dug in on Little Round Top, strengthening the natural defenses with lines of stone breastworks | =That night, Union forces dug in on Little Round Top strengthening the natural defenses with lines of stone breastworks (see Figure 2.50 The Battle of Gettysburg Period Plan).

26 | earthworks | Stone walls dismantled to make breastworks | E-14,E-13,P-14 | 1863-07 | stated | The terrain of the hill was not greatly changed by the battle, except for the dismantling of several stone walls that delineated properties to create stone breastworks stacked rapidly by Union forces immediately before and during battle | =From a physiographic standpoint, the terrain of the hill was not greatly changed by the battle with the exception of the dismantling of several stone walls that delineated properties to create stone breastworks stacked rapidly by Union forces immediately before and during battle (Figure 2.11).
26 | damage | Farm fences destroyed and vegetation trampled and cut down | - | 1863-07 (after the fighting) | stated | There was the inevitable destruction of farm fences and the trampling and cutting down of vegetation that followed a fierce battle | =There was, of course, the inevitable destruction of farm fences and the trampling and cutting down of vegetation that followed a fierce battle.
26 | damage | Fields toward Round Top tramped, no fences, October 1863 | - | ~1863-10 (post-battle letter) | stated | A letter of F. M. Stoke to J. M. Stoke of October 26, 1863 describes the fields toward Round Top as 'tramped like the road, no fences anywhere' | =Typescript of letter of F.M. Stoke to J.M. Stoke, October 26, 1863 is one of the more descriptive post-battle letters. It describes the fields toward Round Top as “tramped like the road, no fences anywhere.”

27 | earthworks | Breastworks restacked 1882-1887 into a more refined appearance | E-14,E-13 | ~1882-1887 (restacking) | stated | The stone breastworks on Little Round Top were restacked between 1882 and 1887, and the rehabilitated walls presented a much more refined appearance than the original battle works and the individual soldier barricades that peppered the boulders on the face of Little Round Top | =The stone breastworks on Little Round Top, for example, were restacked between 1882 and 1887, and the rehabilitated walls presented a much more refined appearance than the original battle works, and individual soldier barricades that peppered the boulders on the face of Little Round Top (Figure 2.17).

29 | earthworks | Warren maps as the source for locating original breastworks and fence lines | E-14,P-14 | ~1868-1869 (Warren survey, used to locate battle-era features) | stated | The Warren maps are still used as the primary source for locating the original breastworks, fence lines, wood lots and other battle-era landscape features at Little Round Top and throughout the park | =The Warren maps are still used as the primary source for locating the original breastworks, fence lines, wood lots and other battle-era landscape features at Little Round Top and throughout the park.

84 | earthworks | Stone breastworks hastily built on the night of July 2 and refined until July 5 | E-14,E-13 | 1863-07-02 to 1863-07-05 | stated | Hastily constructed on the night of July 2, 1863, in preparation for the next day's battle, the low fieldstone barricades gave a modest amount of cover for Union troops defending Little Round Top when battle resumed on the morning of July 3; throughout its occupation until July 5 Union forces extended and refined these stone wall breastworks | =Stone Breastworks: Hastily constructed on the night of July 2, 1863, in preparation for the next day’s battle, these low fieldstone barricades provided a modest amount of cover for Union troops defending Little Round Top when battle resumed on the morning of July 3 (Figures 3.72 and 3.73). Throughout its occupation until July 5, Union forces extended and {{revined|refined}} these stone wall breastworks.
84 | earthworks | Extant breastworks rebuilt or restored several times | E-14,E-13 | ~modern (the breastworks of 2010-11) | stated | Nearly all of the breastworks that are extant today have been rebuilt or restored several times throughout Little Round Top's post-war history, usually in the same location as they appeared during the battle | =Nearly all of the breastworks that are extant today have been rebuilt or restored several times throughout Little Round Top’s post-war history, usually in the same location as they appeared during the battle.
84 | fences | Fieldstone fence with a wood split-rail rider north of Wheatfield Road | P-14 | ~modern (the fence of 2010-11) | hedged | A portion of the fieldstone fence north of Wheatfield Road is topped with a wood split rail fence; the field it enclosed was likely grazing land for livestock | =Fieldstone Fence with Rider: A portion of the fieldstone fence north of Wheatfield Road is topped with a wood split rail fence (Figure 3.71). The field enclosed by this fence was likely grazing land for livestock, as the additional height created by the wood rails would have kept animals from escaping from or intruding on a private field.
